'''Sanjō family''' (さんじょうけ, {{old-style|'''三條家'''}}) is a noble house of the {{ill|Fujiwara clan|ja|藤原氏|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, belonging to the {{ill|Fujiwara Hokke|ja|藤原北家|qq=draft|qq=draft}} {{ill|Kanin lineage|ja|閑院流|qq=draft|qq=draft}} and its {{ill|Main lineage|ja|嫡流|qq=draft|qq=draft}}.{{sfn|Ota|1934|p=2699}}<ref name="lib.u-tokyo">{{Cite web|和書|url= https://www.lib.u-tokyo.ac.jp/ja/library/general/collectionall/sanjo|title=三條公爵家本（三條文庫）|accessdate= 2023-05-11 |author=Tokyo University Library|work= [https://www.lib.u-tokyo.ac.jp/ja Tokyo University Library] |language= 日本語 }}</ref>{{sfn|Kazoku Kakei Daisei Editorial Committee|1996|p=698}}. The family's status as a {{ill|Kuge|ja|公家|1=Kuge|qq=draft|qq=draft}} was {{ill|Seiga family|ja|清華家|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, and as {{ill|Kazoku|ja|華族|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, it held the Rank of {{ill|Duke|ja|公爵|qq=draft|qq=draft}}.{{sfn|Kotabe Yūji|2006|p=57}}. The family crest is {{ill|Karahana crest|ja|唐花紋|qq=draft|qq=draft}} ({{ruby|Katabami ni Karahana|かたばみにからはな}}).{{Sfn|Ota|1934|p=2701}}. The traditional {{ruby|common name|通字}} alternated between "'''Kō''' (公)" and "'''San''' (実)".{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=149}}. The 31st head of the family, {{ill|Sanjō Sanetomi|ja|三条実美|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, is renowned for serving as the last {{ill|Daijō Daijin|ja|太政大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft}} during the early {{ill|Meiji Restoration|ja|明治維新|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, playing a leading role in national politics.<ref name="kotobank"/>

To distinguish itself from the {{ill|Ogyū-Sanjō family|ja|正親町三条家|qq=draft|qq=draft}} (later the {{ill|Saga family|ja|嵯峨家|qq=draft|qq=draft}}), the family was also called the "Tenpōrin Sanjō family" (てんぽうりんさんじょうけ) or simply "Tenpōrin family" (転法輪家).{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335-336}}. 

== History ==

=== Heian Period ===
The {{ill|Kanin lineage|ja|閑院流|qq=draft|qq=draft}} originated from {{ill|Fujiwara no Tadahira|ja|藤原忠平|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, whose second son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Morosuke|ja|藤原師輔|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, had an eleventh son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Kinnori|ja|藤原公季|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, known as the Kanin Daijō Daijin (閑院太政大臣).{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=147}}. 

His grandson, {{ill|Fujiwara no Kinnari|ja|藤原公成|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, became the maternal grandfather of {{ill|Emperor Shirakawa|ja|白河天皇|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, and Kinnari's son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Sanetsugu|ja|藤原実季|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, became the maternal grandfather of {{ill|Emperor Toba|ja|鳥羽天皇|qq=draft|qq=draft}}. Furthermore, Sanetsugu's son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Kinnobu|ja|藤原公実|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, fathered {{ill|Empress Taiken-mon In|ja|藤原璋子|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, who was the mother of both {{ill|Emperor Sutoku|ja|崇徳天皇|qq=draft|qq=draft}} and {{ill|Emperor Go-Shirakawa|ja|Emperor Go-Shirakawa|qq=draft|qq=draft}}. Due to these connections, the Kanin lineage dominated the role of {{ill|Emperor's maternal relatives|ja|外戚|qq=draft|qq=draft}} during the reigns of Emperor Shirakawa and Emperor Toba.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}. 

The Sanjō family traces its origins to {{ill|Sanjō Saneyuki|ja|三条実行|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, the second son of Fujiwara no Kinnobu. Saneyuki, along with his son, {{ill|Sanjō Kinari|ja|三条公教|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, held considerable influence during the rule of Emperor Toba.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}. Saneyuki was promoted to {{ill|Daijō Daijin|ja|太政大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, and he resided in an estate at {{ill|Sanjō Takakura Palace|ja|三条高倉第|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, which led to the adoption of the Sanjō family name.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=147-148}}. 

His grandson, {{ill|Sanjō Sanefusa|ja|三条実房|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, attained the position of {{ill|Sadaijin|ja|左大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft}} and became known as a scholar of court practices.{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=148}}. He is particularly notable for his diary, the "{{ill|Gumaiki|ja|愚昧記|qq=draft|qq=draft}}", a valuable historical record of court affairs.{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=148}}. 

=== Kamakura to Edo Period ===
The Sanjō family continued to hold high court Ranks, sometimes reaching the position of {{ill|Daijō Daijin|ja|太政大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft}}. They became one of the most prominent {{ill|Seiga family|ja|清華家|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, second only to the {{ill|Sekke|ja|摂家|qq=draft|qq=draft}} in status.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=336}}.

