'''Sanjō family''' (さんじょうけ, {{old-style|'''三條家'''}}) is a noble house of the {{ill|Fujiwara clan|ja|藤原氏|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara clan|qq=}}, belonging to the {{ill|Fujiwara Hokke|ja|藤原北家|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara Hokke|qq=}} {{ill|Kanin lineage|ja|閑院流|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kanin lineage|qq=}} and its {{ill|Main lineage|ja|嫡流|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Main lineage|qq=}}.{{sfn|Ota|1934|p=2699}}<ref name="lib.u-tokyo">{{Cite web|和書|url= https://www.lib.u-tokyo.ac.jp/ja/library/general/collectionall/sanjo|title=三條公爵家本（三條文庫）|accessdate= 2023-05-11 |author=Tokyo University Library|work= [https://www.lib.u-tokyo.ac.jp/ja Tokyo University Library] |language= 日本語 }}</ref>{{sfn|Kazoku Kakei Daisei Editorial Committee|1996|p=698}}. The family's status as a {{ill|Kuge|ja|公家|1=Kuge|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kuge|13=User:Immanuelle/Kuge|qq=}} was {{ill|Seiga families|ja|清華家|1=Seiga families|2=ja|3=清華家|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Seiga families|qq=}}, and as {{ill|Kazoku|ja|華族|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kazoku|qq=}}, it held the Rank of {{ill|Duke|ja|公爵|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Duke|qq=}}.{{sfn|Kotabe Yūji|2006|p=57}}. The family crest is {{ill|Karahana crest|ja|唐花紋|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Karahana crest|qq=}} ({{ruby|Katabami ni Karahana|かたばみにからはな}}).{{Sfn|Ota|1934|p=2701}}. The traditional {{ruby|common name|通字}} alternated between "'''Kō''' (公)" and "'''San''' (実)".{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=149}}. The 31st head of the family, {{ill|Sanjō Sanetomi|ja|三条実美|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sanjō Sanetomi|qq=}}, is renowned for serving as the last {{ill|Daijō Daijin|ja|太政大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Daijō Daijin|qq=}} during the early {{ill|Meiji Restoration|ja|明治維新|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiji Restoration|qq=}}, playing a leading role in national politics.<ref name="kotobank"/>

To distinguish itself from the {{ill|Ogyū-Sanjō family|ja|正親町三条家|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ogyū-Sanjō family|qq=}} (later the {{ill|Saga family|ja|嵯峨家|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Saga family|qq=}}), the family was also called the "Tenpōrin Sanjō family" (てんぽうりんさんじょうけ) or simply "Tenpōrin family" (転法輪家).{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335-336}}. 

== History ==

=== Heian Period ===
The {{ill|Kanin lineage|ja|閑院流|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kanin lineage|qq=}} originated from {{ill|Fujiwara no Tadahira|ja|藤原忠平|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Tadahira|qq=}}, whose second son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Morosuke|ja|藤原師輔|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Morosuke|qq=}}, had an eleventh son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Kinnori|ja|藤原公季|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Kinnori|qq=}}, known as the Kanin Daijō Daijin (閑院太政大臣).{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=147}}. 

His grandson, {{ill|Fujiwara no Kinnari|ja|藤原公成|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Kinnari|qq=}}, became the maternal grandfather of {{ill|Emperor Shirakawa|ja|白河天皇|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Emperor Shirakawa|qq=}}, and Kinnari's son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Sanetsugu|ja|藤原実季|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Sanetsugu|qq=}}, became the maternal grandfather of {{ill|Emperor Toba|ja|鳥羽天皇|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Emperor Toba|qq=}}. Furthermore, Sanetsugu's son, {{ill|Fujiwara no Kinnobu|ja|藤原公実|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Kinnobu|qq=}}, fathered {{ill|Empress Taiken-mon In|ja|藤原璋子|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Empress Taiken-mon In|qq=}}, who was the mother of both {{ill|Emperor Sutoku|ja|崇徳天皇|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Emperor Sutoku|qq=}} and {{ill|Emperor Go-Shirakawa|ja|Emperor Go-Shirakawa|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Emperor Go-Shirakawa|qq=}}. Due to these connections, the Kanin lineage dominated the role of {{ill|Emperor's maternal relatives|ja|外戚|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Emperor's maternal relatives|qq=}} during the reigns of Emperor Shirakawa and Emperor Toba.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}. 

The Sanjō family traces its origins to {{ill|Sanjō Saneyuki|ja|三条実行|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sanjō Saneyuki|qq=}}, the second son of Fujiwara no Kinnobu. Saneyuki, along with his son, {{ill|Sanjō Kinari|ja|三条公教|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sanjō Kinari|qq=}}, held considerable influence during the rule of Emperor Toba.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}. Saneyuki was promoted to {{ill|Daijō Daijin|ja|太政大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Daijō Daijin|qq=}}, and he resided in an estate at {{ill|Sanjō Takakura Palace|ja|三条高倉第|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sanjō Takakura Palace|qq=}}, which led to the adoption of the Sanjō family name.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=335}}{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=147-148}}. 

His grandson, {{ill|Sanjō Sanefusa|ja|三条実房|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sanjō Sanefusa|qq=}}, attained the position of {{ill|Sadaijin|ja|左大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sadaijin|qq=}} and became known as a scholar of court practices.{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=148}}. He is particularly notable for his diary, the "{{ill|Gumaiki|ja|愚昧記|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gumaiki|qq=}}", a valuable historical record of court affairs.{{sfn|Hashimoto Masanobu|2010|p=148}}. 

=== Kamakura to Edo Period ===
The Sanjō family continued to hold high court Ranks, sometimes reaching the position of {{ill|Daijō Daijin|ja|太政大臣|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Daijō Daijin|qq=}}. They became one of the most prominent {{ill|Seiga families|ja|清華家|1=Seiga families|2=ja|3=清華家|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Seiga families|qq=}}, second only to the {{ill|Sekke|ja|摂家|1=Five regent houses|1=Five regent houses|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sekke|qq=}} in status.{{sfn|Suzuki Keizō|1995|p=336}}.

